WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.009 --> 00:00:02.130
The Montessori method was developed by Dr.

1
00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:05.349
Maria Montessori in the early 20th century.

2
00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:08.560
This approach is built on child-led exploration,

3
00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:09.920
prepared environments,

4
00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:11.369
and hands-on learning.

5
00:00:11.689 --> 00:00:14.239
Instead of using traditional direct instruction.

6
00:00:14.439 --> 00:00:16.520
The Montessori approach allows learning

7
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.739
to happen through self-directed activity

8
00:00:18.959 --> 00:00:20.750
within multi-age environments.

9
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.159
Children choose from carefully designed,

10
00:00:23.399 --> 00:00:28.920
self-correcting materials like holding a wooden cube of 1000 beads to physically

11
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.080
feel what 1000 means before ever writing the symbol on paper.

12
00:00:33.479 --> 00:00:36.560
These tangible experiences build mental models that

13
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.299
naturally transition children from concrete physical understanding

14
00:00:40.439 --> 00:00:42.029
to abstract concepts.

15
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.790
Montessori emphasizes uninterrupted blocks of work time,

16
00:00:45.959 --> 00:00:47.830
fostering deep concentration,

17
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:48.880
independence,

18
00:00:49.159 --> 00:00:50.200
self-regulation,

19
00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:51.799
and internal motivation.

20
00:00:52.099 --> 00:00:54.360
The teacher serves as a trained guide,

21
00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.139
observing each child's developmental readiness and introducing

22
00:00:58.139 --> 00:01:00.099
new materials as the child is ready.

23
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.300
Through respect for the child's natural pace and intrinsic curiosity,

24
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:07.489
Montessori aims to cultivate independent thinkers,

25
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.260
confident problem solvers,

26
00:01:09.419 --> 00:01:10.819
and lifelong learners.